class Solution {
    public int[] twoSum(int[] numbers, int target) {
        /*1-indexxed array of ints, already sorted in non-decereasing order (increasing), find 2 nums that add up to target & return their indeces as an int array of len 2*/


        /*will use 2 pointers, left and right, as the bounds for an upcoming loop. want to use binary search to find the rightmost boundary (potentially cutting down on RT), then doing regular subtraction (target - currIdx) and storing result. result is stored in a hashmap, where key = num already viewed in array that has result of the subtration, and val = result of subtraction done previously (for 2 numbers to add up to target, they both must exist in the array, so yeah) */
        HashMap<Integer, Integer> memory = new HashMap<>();
        int result = 0;
        int[] answer = new int[]{0,0};
        //forget binary search, worst case RT is O(N)
        //assuming we always get number.length >= 2

        for(int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++)
        {
            result = target - numbers[i];
            if(memory.containsKey(result))
            {
                answer = new int[]{memory.get(result),i+1};
            }
            //otherwise
            //key = #, val = idx
            memory.put(numbers[i], i+1);
        }
        return answer;
        /*so the biggest bug came from the indexing. the java array itself isn't 1 indexed, but we had to pretend it is, so the boundaries are 0 indexed (0 - len-1), but the answer and memory needs to treat it like its 1 indexed, hence why we have +1 for adding vals to memory and in the answer[] */
    }
}